Date: Tue,  7 Sep 1993 20:20:48 -0400 (EDT)
From: Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

The reason why I define "rights" the way I do is to distinguish it from
the idea of "being nice" or "having respect for". I'm stripping it of
all previous implications when I define it this way, so that not having
rights does not take away a being's deserving of life, respect, etc, and
does *not* necessarily make it worthless vermin. The exchange of rights,
as I define it, is a contract between two individuals, probably
implicit. A right is two-sided: you can't *exchange* a right with
something that doesn't understand it! Therefore, when you grant someone
a right, you assume 1) they understand the concept of a right, or at
least the concept of whatever the right is, i.e. property; and 2) you
expect the grantee to give you the same rights in return. Otherwise, it
is an unfair trade. I therefore make a philosophical distinction between
granting someone (or something) a "right" to life, and having respect
for its life. This is also why I keep saying that having rights does not
necessarily make one *better* than someone that does not have rights. I
simply want to distinguish the concepts.

Working with _this_ supposedly valid distinction, then, it is impossible
to grant rights to something that can not understand what a right is.
For example, you may say your pet cat has a right to existence, but it
doesn't: it doesn't know what life is, because it doesn't know what
death (its own, anyway) is. Therefore, it can't respect *your* "right"
to existence, if you grant it said right. It only has a "right" (a la
definition, not whatever it implies) to existence as a corollary to your
right to property (the cat.) The cat "has every right" (per popular
definition, i.e., deserves) to live unmolested, but it does not have the
_right_ (conscious & recognized) to do so. This is *not* discrimination.
The cat simply can't help it.

You talk facetiously about being a squirrel, but that really doesn't
help the argument: as a squirrel, etc., you couldn't be having this
discussion. You would be unfettered by the trivial concepts of rights,
higher/lower beings, and other such  philosophy. You would be simply
what you were, and as long as you were unmolested, you'd go about your
day. You would not, in other words, be able to--or want to--fill the
other half of a "rights" contract.

My point is, I guess, that "rights" as an idea arises as a *social
contract* between individuals of an intelligent species, to deal with
other intelligent beings in the **most constructive** (key word)
possible way. To grant rights to animals and make an exception for their
inability to reciprocate those rights may seem "humane", but it strikes
me as a form of unjustified altruism--and it defeats the purpose of the
"rights" contract, because it's not constructive.

This is not constructive because when we begin to give attention and
social precedence to beings that can't (or won't) contribute back to
society, it begins to interfere with the production of those that can
and do contribute. For example, an architect finds the perfect spot to
build a house, and works a contract with the land's owner to buy it. He
draws up fantastic plans. When the bulldozers begin to roll, however, a
squirrel comes charging out of a knothole, and shakes its tiny fist at
the noise.

Squirrel: "Graarrrararaaara!!" (squeaky, hard to do in English.)

Architect: "Oh, sir, I'm sorry! Do you live here?"

Squirrel: "Rarraragrara! *squeak* *sneeze*"

Architect: "Oh, it seems you do. I'm terribly sorry. May I offer you a
contract of some kind...? Perhaps a better home? Custom-built?"

Squirrel: "Grah!!!" It scampers back into its hole, frightened at the
architect's approach.

Architect: "Um... uh... oh, damn it. My whole dream is ruined. I can't
oust that poor innocent squirrel. It has rights to its property, just
like I do. And I can't build around that tree. I've lost a small fortune
on this! My only hope is that the owner'll buy back the land (around the
tree, of course)..."

As a wise man once said, "There's something skewey awound here."

And, I suppose, there is your answer to the property rights question.
The squirrel has no regard for your or my property; they scamper all
over the CMU campus without a care in the world for the school's
property rights and the fact that they are trespassing. The same with
the pigeons around here. *Why should we recognize the squirel's
property, if it won't recognize ours?*

To me, this is neither arbitrary nor rationalized. Comments? Objections?

BTW, F.E.S., I have to admit to being kind of confused by your
disclaimers that you don't presume to hold any kind of objective truth.
Why do you hold this opinion, as opposed to another, if you don't think
you're right?

Ratha                                      

