>	Are you familiar with Julian Simon's writings on the subject?
>Maybe you should check into it.  Usable resources become more plentiful
>as technology progresses.  The chicken little environmentalists who keep
>(falsly) predicting doom around the corner because our "resources are
>running out" are dead wrong.

I need to go check Julian Simon's stuff - can you suggest a title? But it  
seems to me that its dangerously simplistic to assume that "Usable resources  
[always] become more plentiful as technology progresses." I'm not denying  
that they don't often do, or perhaps even usually, but it would be silly to  
assume that its always the case. I can imagine many examples (both gedanken  
and historical) where the necessary technical jump was too great, or resource  
depletion had got to the point where there wasn't enough to maintain  
*current* tech levels.

Here's a couple:

Easter Island and perhaps other island people who used all possible  
boat-building materials and had their culture decline both sociologically and  
technologically.

Mayan culture which apparently used high-yield farm land for urban use and  
grew beyond the ability of their food transport system to support their  
population, with catastrophic results.

***

I think that we often *can* open up new usable resourses by advancing our  
technology, but I'm not so filled with hubris as to think that we aren't  
capable of painting ourselves into a corner, resource-wise. Thinking you  
can't goof big is a good way to goof bigger.

Humans often appear to adress resourse depletion problems by moving elsewhere  
(ie. Siberian land bridge of the last ice age, the exploration of the New  
World by Europeans in part because they'd managed to burn down most of their  
trees, the same folks going into Africa around the same time). Needless to  
say - although this often works, it can lead to alot of human misery and  
waste of resources and time spent fighting over same.

Note that all the above doesn't mean that I think that the way to adress  
planetary and regional resourse management is to give the power to some  
"State" (as apparently many people who call themselves "environmentalists"  
do). In large part, I believe its a bug in the whole "State" meme that causes  
most of these shortages and subsequent human suffering (economically,  
politically, and spiritually). So I differ from most "environmentalists" in  
that I think the answer does not lie in increased involvement by the "State".

We just need to find ways of addressing the sane and efficient management of  
said resourses that don't have the disadvantages that using "States" does. So  
I look to anarcho-capitalism and distributed market democracy to achieve my  
"environmentalist" goals. Since I believe in the market, I beleive that the  
market will "reward" those who use resources efficiently with ultimately  
lower costs and increased competitiveness. Its the top-heavy "state"  
mechanisms and bureaucracy that allow companies to be wasteful and "cheat".  
If you assume Big Brother is watching out for you, then he and Big Business  
can screw you behind your back. If you aren't depending on Big Brother, you  
watch your own back.

Flame off.

 -Jeff

