    The F. E. S. confesses that the thrust of his comments about
advanced beings was to illustrate the interaction of what he terms the
"always-somebody-faster" principle with behavioral codes based on
exploitation.  (He repeats that no moral judgement of such codes is
intended.)  Thus it seems likely that repeated interaction between
entities using the summarized rule would result in a field of play
containing exactly one exploiter, with all other original players
ending up resources.  It seems but an accident of evolution that Earth
is presently populated with a substantial number of "winning" players
of roughly equal competence, so that there is at least some
distribution of (other) resources among one species.
    The widespread Extropian confidence in the imminence of human
transformation, upload and/or space exploration would appear to have
as corollary that this equilibrium is unlikely to last.  In the near
future, we might encounter humans of capability enhanced to differing
degrees, or uploaded humans of varying capability, or AIs of our own
manufacture, or nonterrestrial entities who passed through that
primitive stage of evolution that we presently revere as "upload",
several billion years ago.  Barring astronomically improbable good
luck, most or all terrestrial entities might thus look forward to a
useful and productive future as something else's raw materials.
    For all the intrinsic interest of the debate, The F. E. S. submits
that anything we speculate upon or discuss here is unlikely to weigh
heavily with an advanced exploiter, of whatever origin.  We are but
whistling in the dark.  The F. E. S. will cheerfully plug on
regardless -- squirrels are not given to brooding -- but if the
summarized rule is widespread, he has high statistical confidence in
his ultimate doom at the appendage-equivalents of whatever the most
successful exploiter of resources in the cosmos turns out to be.

    Perhaps his only hope is that it will have chosen to be
considerate of animals.

                            -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

