Extraordinarily unlikely. Indeed, I see no reason at all to believe
this. 

a) It assumes that the best species has only one member.
b) it assumes that this one member is omnipotent

Remember, even humans can only exploit a small amount of territory.

Besides, evolution has favored cooperative interaction between
creatures capable of cooperating. When you and I trade, the division
of labor allows both of us to be more efficient than either of us
could be alone.

> It seems but an accident of evolution that Earth
> is presently populated with a substantial number of "winning" players
> of roughly equal competence, so that there is at least some
> distribution of (other) resources among one species.

Why would you think of this as an accident? Seems to me to be part of
the nature of things.

>     The widespread Extropian confidence in the imminence of human
> transformation, upload and/or space exploration would appear to have
> as corollary that this equilibrium is unlikely to last.  In the near
> future, we might encounter humans of capability enhanced to differing
> degrees, or uploaded humans of varying capability, or AIs of our own
> manufacture, or nonterrestrial entities who passed through that
> primitive stage of evolution that we presently revere as "upload",
> several billion years ago.  Barring astronomically improbable good
> luck, most or all terrestrial entities might thus look forward to a
> useful and productive future as something else's raw materials.

1) See comparative advantage for a reason that this would not be the
   case. Squirrels cannot negotiate contracts -- humans can, even with
   superior humans.
2) It is unlikely that uploads will be hunting ANYTHING for food, and
   it is also unlikely that uploads will need primitive biological
   sources of chemicals. I suspect that the "Raw materials" notion is
   extremely unlikely.

